Queue microphone buffers instead of replacing the one that hasn't been read yet.

Applied by .engine/tools/build-ffmpeg.sh to FFmpeg n9.0.2. The lines above the
first "diff" are a note; git apply skips them.

FFmpeg's avfoundation input keeps a single audio buffer. The capture callback
replaces it whether or not it has been read, and ffmpeg asks for it every 10 ms
or so (it sleeps 10 ms each time there is nothing to read). A microphone at
48 kHz delivers a 512-frame buffer every 10.7 ms, so about one buffer in nine
is replaced before it is read: 11% of a 32-minute call recorded through
AirPods in their 48 kHz mode. A MacBook Air's own microphone at 44.1 kHz
(11.6 ms a buffer) lost 7% in a 30-second test. Nothing is logged, and the
timestamps keep pace with the clock. At 24 kHz a buffer lasts 21 ms and
nothing is lost.

Tested with a 30-second capture from that MacBook Air microphone, idle and
with every core busy: 92.7% and 93% of the audio without the patch, 100% with
it both times.

With this patch a buffer that arrives while another is waiting goes into a
queue, and the reader takes them in order. The queue holds up to 512 buffers
(over 5 seconds at 48 kHz) so memory stays bounded if the reader stops; past
that a buffer is dropped, and this time it is logged.

diff --git a/libavdevice/avfoundation.m b/libavdevice/avfoundation.m
index ebec1ac..41d70b2 100644
--- a/libavdevice/avfoundation.m
+++ b/libavdevice/avfoundation.m
@@ -132,6 +132,8 @@
     AVCaptureAudioDataOutput *audio_output;
     CMSampleBufferRef         current_frame;
     CMSampleBufferRef         current_audio_frame;
+    CFMutableArrayRef         audio_queue;         ///< audio buffers waiting behind current_audio_frame
+    int                       audio_frames_dropped;
 
     AVCaptureDevice          *observed_device;
 #if !TARGET_OS_IPHONE && __MAC_OS_X_VERSION_MIN_REQUIRED >= 1070
@@ -150,6 +152,27 @@ static void unlock_frames(AVFContext* ctx)
     pthread_mutex_unlock(&ctx->frame_lock);
 }
 
+/**
+ * Audio buffers that arrive while an earlier one is still waiting to be read
+ * are queued, not dropped. avf_read_packet() is polled (ffmpeg sleeps 10 ms
+ * when it returns EAGAIN), and a 48 kHz device delivers a 512-frame buffer
+ * every 10.7 ms, so with a single slot about one buffer in nine was replaced
+ * before it was read. The limit only bounds memory if the reader stops:
+ * 512 buffers are over 5 seconds of 48 kHz audio.
+ */
+#define AVF_AUDIO_QUEUE_MAX 512
+
+/** Make the oldest queued audio buffer the current one. Call with the frame lock held. */
+static void next_audio_frame(AVFContext* ctx)
+{
+    ctx->current_audio_frame = nil;
+
+    if (ctx->audio_queue && CFArrayGetCount(ctx->audio_queue) > 0) {
+        ctx->current_audio_frame = (CMSampleBufferRef)CFRetain(CFArrayGetValueAtIndex(ctx->audio_queue, 0));
+        CFArrayRemoveValueAtIndex(ctx->audio_queue, 0);
+    }
+}
+
